# Line-ending normalisation.
#
# Without this, a clone on Windows with core.autocrlf=true gets CRLF in every text file. That is
# not only cosmetic: a shell script checked out with CRLF and then copied into a Linux container
# fails to run at all ("bad interpreter: no such file or directory"), and CRLF in a Dockerfile
# ENTRYPOINT or an SQL migration produces equally confusing failures. Every tracked text file is
# currently LF, so adding this changes nothing today and keeps it that way.

# Normalise to LF in the repository, check out native on Windows for ordinary text.
* text=auto

# Files that must be LF in the working tree too, because they are executed by Linux tooling
# (containers, CI) regardless of the developer's operating system.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.bash          text eol=lf
Dockerfile      text eol=lf
*.Dockerfile    text eol=lf
.dockerignore   text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.sql           text eol=lf

# Windows-only scripts keep CRLF.
*.ps1           text eol=crlf
*.cmd           text eol=crlf
*.bat           text eol=crlf

# Generated lockfiles: keep them diffable but never let a merge tool reformat them.
pnpm-lock.yaml  text eol=lf -diff
package-lock.json text eol=lf -diff

# Binary assets — never touch these.
*.png    binary
*.jpg    binary
*.jpeg   binary
*.gif    binary
*.webp   binary
*.ico    binary
*.svgz   binary
*.pdf    binary
*.jar    binary
*.woff   binary
*.woff2  binary
*.ttf    binary
*.eot    binary
